  "reactionVersion": 2,
  "mechanismSummary": "Ahrendt, Borths and MacMillan (2000) named the idea organocatalysis. A chiral secondary amine condenses reversibly with an α,β-unsaturated aldehyde to give an iminium ion: the amine adds with acid help, the proton moves to water, and H₃O⁺ helps water leave as the nitrogen pushes. The iminium ion is a far more reactive dienophile than the enal, because the positive nitrogen lowers the LUMO of the C=C (as a Lewis acid would). Cyclopentadiene adds in one concerted step to the face not shielded by the catalyst's benzyl group, and water then hydrolyses the iminium ion to the aldehyde and frees the amine. The bicyclic aldehydes form in 99 % yield (exo : endo 1.3 : 1) and 93 % ee. The app does not check stereochemistry yet, so the drawings carry no wedges and either face is accepted.",
